# The composed layers whose remainder has no figure at all. One row per composed layer whose own
# totals do not give its remainder and whose remainder from the parts is not owed.
#
# Two ways to have a remainder, and neither is open
#   A composed layer's remainder is its own nameplate less its demand, from the totals, where that
#   is legitimate, or the remainder worked out from its parts where that is owed.
#   composition/unsettled.sqlc names the layers where the first is not legitimate: a conversion
#   factor that is a range scales both totals, so subtracting them counts the factor's range twice
#   (`asrt:Part`: a converted remainder is never worked out again).
#   composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc names the layers where the second is suspended. A layer in
#   both has no figure the model can stand behind, and falling back to the totals would hand every
#   remainder rule the figure `asrt:Part` refuses.
#
# A set of its own, for the depth of the plan
#   layers/remainder.sqlc drops these rows, and arithmetic/remainder.sqlc says why. A filter there
#   that tested the join for the parts' remainder itself would plan every rule above it several
#   levels deeper, past what a plan read back when those statements are checked at compile time can
#   nest; testing against this set does not.
-- composition/unsettled.sqlc within composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc.
SELECT o.filing, o.layer
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/unsettled.sqlc)
) o
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/suspended_remainders.sqlc)
) l ON l.composition = o.filing AND l.composed_layer = o.layer
